\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 +\'80(void)\b load\
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 This method is invoked whenever a class or category is added to the Objective-C runtime; implement this method to perform class-specific behavior upon loading. It is sent to classes and categories that are both dynamically loaded and statically linked, but only if the newly-loaded class or category implements a method that can respond. As an example, when Interface Builder loads a palette, the load method is sent to each class and category in the palette.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b load\b0  is usually invoked before \b initialize\b0 . It is usually the very first method sent to the class, although this isn\'27t guaranteed. The order in which classes are loaded is also not guaranteed, to the point that superclasses aren\'27t even guaranteed to be loaded before all of their subclasses. Because you can\'27t rely on other classes being loaded at the point when your class is sent a \b load\b0  message, you should be extremely careful when messaging other classes from within your load method.\
\fs14\
\pard \s67 \li2721 \fi-1209 \ri1007 \ql \tx2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Warning:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 Due to the amount of uncertainty about the environment at the point that \b load\b0  is invoked, you should avoid using \b load\b0  whenever possible. All class-specific initialization should be done in the class\'27s \b initialize\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Note that although \b load\b0  is essentially replaces NEXTSTEP\'27s \b finishLoading:\b0  method, the circumstances surrounding their invocation is slightly different. Consult your NEXTSTEP Developer documentation if you are porting code that uses \b finishLoading:\b0 .\
\fs14\
